00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.590 align:start position:0%
 
so hi everyone thank you for  coming to listen uh we are from lehigh  university and we're going to be talking  about our project  in stem learning through visualization  and that we have run through the office  of creative inquiry at lehigh university  and all the work we talk about today can  be found on  our website and all the code can be  found on our github repository link here  so i am anna marini i am a senior at  lehigh university and i'm studying  statistics  um my name is brian lucas and i'm a  junior at lehigh university studying  chemical engineering  um the advisors who are directly  associated with this project  are professor rangarajan from the  department of chemical engineering at  lehigh  um dr tiago rajan who  has been of immense help with us but he  is not  affiliated with the university in any  way and then the other  um person who has been of a men's health  to us is the late professor grossi who  is from the  um lehigh university chemical  engineering department  uh we also had some assistance from  people outside of our project  so dr angela brown dr laurie hurst and  professor bill scheissler are all lehigh  university professors  and steve anthony from the lehigh  technical services have all helped us in  some way with our project  so the reason why we took on this  project  was anecdotally speaking from my own  experience  stem subjects and people in stem majors  there is a  fairly low retention rate for people in  stem fields  so a lot of people tend to change majors  pretty early on  um you know who are studying some  subjects  and then with that there's also a  lack of access to experiences inside  labs  and so given the current situation with  coven 19  the accessibility to labs is even more  restricted  so by doing this project we're able to  kind of  enhance the students ability to  personally interact with material  and give access to  you know that interactive experience  that they might not be able to have  so so far we have three models that are  currently being hosted on our website  that people can access  the first model is a reaction kinetics  model for sequential reactions  the second model is a thermodynamic and  heat transfer model that deals with a  zero energy cooling chamber  and the third model that we have  currently hosted on our website is an  infectious disease model  that details the spread of an infectious  disease  so i'm going to walk you through our  infectious disease model a little bit  more  so it's an scir infectious disease model  which is based on a system of  differential equations  so for our model we have eight classes  and therefore eight differential  equations  and our classes are susceptible people  exposed and then we have four infected  classes  recovered and dead and the four infected  classes  we separated them out due to different  potential rates  so we have in asymptomatic and unknown  infected  in asymptomatic and known infected  symptomatic  infected and non-hospitalized  symptomatic infected and hospitalized  so when you open up the  sphere model on our website this is what  you see and on the left we have a  network graph  and on the right is a bar graph both of  the  class populations for each of the eight  classes  and then there's this time slider with a  pause and play button  so when the user hits the play button  the program will start iterating through  time and show how  each of the class populations changes  and the user can pause this and drag the  time slider to a specific value  if they want and these graphs also have  some interesting capabilities that we  are able to add so if a user hovers over  an  edge a description will pop out  describing how an individual moves from  one class to another along that edge  and on the bar graph the user can zoom  in  and they can hover over the bars to get  the current class populations  and then we also just have some  additional information below  so under the second tab which is a sort  of build your own infectious disease  where the user has the ability to change  different parameter values such as  infection rate recovery rate or  introduce a vaccine  and the graph will automatically update  to reflect these parameters and changes  so the top graph has all eight of the  class populations over time  and the bottom graph just displays the  four infectious classes to get a more  zoomed in look and then the table on the  right just displays all the current  parameter values being used  for the current model displayed the user  can also  hover over parts of these graphs to get  population values at the specific time  on the x axis  and then we included one final tab just  with some further information about the  processes  involved in this model so i'm also just  going to explain a little bit more about  how we  were able to use the bouquet package in  python to  include these interactive aspects in our  model  and so it's kind of a cycle and i'm just  going to include some of the code  snippets and talk you through  so the first step was setting up the  data and the initial conditions so this  code on the right is just  a bunch of the initial parameter values  we had to set  to have our base models to build off of  and then the code below  below is setting up the network graph so  creating all of the nodes and their  initial sizes  and location and creating the edges  between certain nodes  so the second step is plotting these  initial conditions like i mentioned  the infectious disease model is based  off a system of differential equations  so the solve ivp function solves this  system of differential equations over  the time span which we use  of 365 days um and  this stored each of the class will be  stored  in a vector um that includes each of  their  population values through the 365 days  in time  and then this here also includes setting  up the time  slider and setting the initial sizes  of the nodes  so then the last big aspect of this is  how the user can interact with the model  and the code  that supports that so this first  function on the right  update data bubble gets the current time  at the time slider and then retrieves  each of the eight  class populations at that given time and  updates the network grab and the bar  graph to display the current class  population  and then the next two functions on the  left here kind of deal with the pause  and play button  so the animate update function  automatically iterates through time so  it'll take a time  value display each of the eight class  populations accordingly  and then automatically move to the next  time unit and display those  again and again so then that's used  within  the animate function which calls  the animate update function to stop or  start iterating  when a user presses the pause or play  button  so as mentioned previously we do have  two other models currently being  provided on our website so this is the  um dashboard that one looks at when  you're doing the  reaction kinetics model so on the left  are the sliders where you can adjust the  different values and so the graphs  automatically update as well so it  allows you to basically see the  concentration over time  for two sequential reactions  the next model that we have is a model  for zero energy cooling chamber which is  a cheap and effective way  for storing food and it is um  based off of evaporative cooling that  passively cools food on  the inside of this chamber  so this is the dashboard that one would  look at bearing the zero energy cooling  chamber model  so with this you are able to view  data that we provide on the dashboard  and so students are able to kind of use  this  in while using the model to help  guide their decision making process  while using this  so as you can see there are sliders and  adjustable values and parameters and  then in the bottom there's a table where  you can tabulate  data and keep a record of  of each parameter value as you're  adjusting it  so in this process of making these  models we also took um  surveys of students to get an  understanding of how the user experience  was  so overall it was a very um positive  result that we received so the  overwhelming majority of people found  our models to be clear and useful and  they understood the meaning of it  and furthermore every single person who  we surveyed was actually able to access  the models as presented on the website  from the devices that they used  all right so thank you everyone for  listening to our talk today  we are excited and eager to answer any  questions or comments you may have about  the specific models or projects in  general in the q a portion of this talk  thank you

